{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Persistence of bacterial and archaeal communities in sea ice through an Arctic winter",
    "date": "2026-07-28",
    "lesson": "In Franklin Bay, first-year Arctic sea ice was sampled weekly through the winter darkness of January-March, as brine temperatures fell from -9C to -26C. Despite this severe cold and a significant drop in microbial abundance, the bacterial and archaeal community's richness and composition stayed remarkably stable through the winter. Ice and under-ice seawater alike stayed dominated by SAR11 Alphaproteobacteria and Marine Group I Crenarchaeota, plus oligotrophic Gammaproteobacteria and the sea-ice specialist Polaribacter - none of the genera typical of later-season ice. This suggests that neither selective entrapment during autumn freeze-up nor winter die-off strongly reshapes the community: the distinctive spring/summer sea-ice assemblage must instead arise later, through succession after winter's end. The lesson: survival in brine channels through months of extreme cold and darkness is less about who dies and more about who simply persists.",
    "citation": "Collins, Rocap, Deming (2010). Persistence of bacterial and archaeal communities in sea ice through an Arctic winter. doi:10.1111/j.1462-2920.2010.02179.x",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Franklin Bay, Western Arctic, first-year winter sea ice, Jan-Mar 2004"
  },
